Based on these tests in Table 6 and 7, SES does not moderate gender differences within school types.

## Conclusion

This analyses revealed that there are no statistically significant gender differences in mean reading scores in this dataset. Further, gender differences do not appear to be related to the type of school attended (public or private), as reading scores for males and females were similar across both settings. Finally, socioeconomic status (SES) does not seem to moderate gender differences within school types, as reading scores across gender remained consistent regardless of SES level. Overall, these results suggest that gender, school type, and SES do not meaningfully impact differences in reading scores in this dataset.
